// What a matrix's cells have to be — and the one thing about them the library cannot say.
//
// Linear algebra needs a *field*: a type that adds, subtracts, multiplies, divides, negates, has an
// additive and a multiplicative identity, and can be compared. Nine of those ten arrive as core
// traits, and `Zero` and `One` arrive because a generic body cannot spell an identity any other way —
// `0.0` is a `real` and nothing else, so a body shared by two element types has to *ask*.
//
// The tenth is the pivot. Gaussian elimination chooses the largest remaining cell in a column and
// treats one that is small enough as absent, so it needs a **magnitude**: an ordering on size that is
// not an ordering on values. The complexes have no `<` at all — the library refuses `Ord` for them on
// purpose, because there is no order on the plane that respects arithmetic — and yet `|z|` is exactly
// what pivoting wants. So the requirement is a magnitude and never an `Ord`, and that is why this
// trait exists rather than a bound written out of core traits alone.
//
// WHY IT IS THE GUIDE'S AND NOT THE LIBRARY'S. A magnitude has to say what it answers *with*, and the
// honest answer is "a type the implementation chooses" — `real` here, but a rational matrix would want
// a rational and an interval matrix an interval. sysl has no associated type, so a library trait would
// have to fix the result for everybody, and fixing it to `real` is a floating-point commitment made on
// behalf of element types that have no floating point in them. A guide may commit where a library may
// not: everything below is `real` because everything below is checked with a tolerance.

import sysl.math.Float
import sysl.math.complex.Complex

// The element type of a vector or a matrix. Every requirement is used: `gauss.sysl` divides, negates,
// compares against a zero and multiplies pivots into a determinant, and `text` renders.
//
// `Display` is not algebra and is here anyway, because a numerical type that cannot be printed is a
// numerical type nobody can debug — and a bound is where a generic body says what it needs, whatever
// the need is for.
trait Scalar: Add + Sub + Mul + Div + Neg + Zero + One + Eq + Display
    magnitude(self) -> real

impl Scalar for real
    magnitude(self) -> real = self.abs()

// The same body at the narrower width, converted at the end rather than at the start: `f32.abs()` is
// an `f32`, and widening a magnitude that has already been taken loses nothing.
impl Scalar for f32
    magnitude(self) -> real = real(self.abs())

// The case the whole exercise is for. `Complex[F].abs()` answers an `F` — the modulus is a *real*
// number however the parts are stored — so this is one conversion and no arithmetic, and it works at
// every float width the library has.
impl[F: Float + Display] Scalar for Complex[F]
    magnitude(self) -> real = real(self.abs())
